--TEST--
`Core\Socket::upgrade` accepts `rule:security/isolate-shares-nothing`'s second entry spelling — a static method reference — and it reaches the same body the path form does rather than any compile question — ADR 0083 §§ 1-2
--FILE--
<?nvs
// ADR 0083 § 2 makes the operand `spawn script`'s, and ADR 0006 § *Decision*
// gives that operand two spellings: a file path, and a static method whose
// parameters `args:` binds by name. This case is the accepted half of the rule
// at this second site; `an-upgrade-refuses-an-entry-it-cannot-see-the-function-
// behind.nvst` is the refused half, and the two together are the whole of it.
class Chat {
    public static function run(string $room): void {
        echo "ran ", $room, "\n";
    }
}

// A script run from the command line arrived on no connection, so what says
// the entry was *accepted* is that the member reaches its own body and reports
// the missing connection from there — byte for byte the report the path form
// gets, never a diagnostic and never a silent `void`. The connection is asked
// about before the entry is read, which is why both spellings answer alike;
// `nvs_stdlib::socket`'s § *The method form waits on a name* owns what this
// form still cannot prepare once there is a slot to prepare it into.
try {
    Core\Socket::upgrade(Chat::run(...), args: {room: "lobby"});
    echo "upgraded\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

// The entry is *named*, not called: `ran lobby` is nowhere above, because the
// isolate § 1 opens is the only place that body ever runs.
echo "not run here\n";
--EXPECT--
`Core\Socket::upgrade` needs a connection to upgrade and this request arrived on none: only a request a server framed an upgrade for is offered one
not run here
